<BaseLayout title="Ear Acupressure Points" description="Explore auricular ear acupressure points including Shenmen and Zero Point. Learn how ear reflexology works, key point locations, and benefits for pain and stress." breadcrumbs={[{"label":"Home","href":"/"},{"label":"Body Atlas","href":"/atlas/"},{"label":"Ear","href":"/body/ear-acupressure-points/"}]}>
<article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
  <div class="text-center mb-10">
    <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-forest-600 dark:text-forest-300">Body Region</span>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mt-2">Ear</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-2">Auricular acupressure uses points on the outer ear to influence the whole body. This guide covers five common ear points and the safe, gentle technique to use them at home.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mb-10 flex justify-center"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-32 h-36"><ellipse cx="50" cy="50" rx="20" ry="30" fill="rgba(45,106,79,0.06)" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1.5"></ellipse><ellipse cx="50" cy="48" rx="10" ry="18" fill="none" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="0.8" opacity="0.4"></ellipse><circle cx="50" cy="38" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><circle cx="50" cy="62" r="2" fill="#2d6a4f" opacity="0.5"></circle><text x="50" y="105" font-family="system-ui" font-size="10" fill="#2d6a4f" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">Ear</text></svg></div>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Ear Acupressure Points Matter</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">In traditional practice the ear is treated as a microsystem, meaning each part is thought to correspond to other areas of the body. That is why auricular acupressure, sometimes called auriculotherapy, is a popular self-care choice for people managing anxiety, insomnia, stress, and everyday aches. Classical Chinese practice has long used points such as Shenmen and the Hunger Point for emotional balance and appetite regulation, and our condition guides for anxiety and insomnia refer back to these ear points. This region page pairs ear-specific technique with the management steps already covered across the site.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">From a traditional perspective, ear points connect to the whole body through meridian pathways, which is why a small surface area can feel relevant to complaints elsewhere. In modern terms, the ear carries branches of the vagus and other cranial nerves, so steady, light pressure may support relaxation and ease muscle tension. No single mechanism is proven, and responses vary, but both layers help explain why ear self-care feels calming to many people.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Key Points in This Region</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mb-5">These five auricular points are common starting points for ear acupressure at home. Each card links to full location, method, and cautions.</p>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900">
        <div class="shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-lg bg-forest-100 dark:bg-forest-900/40 flex items-center justify-center text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300 text-sm font-bold">Shenmen</div>
        <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Shenmen (Spirit Gate)</h3>
          <div class="text-yellow-500 text-xs mt-0.5">★★★★★</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-0.5">Anxiety, pain, addiction, insomnia</p>
        </div>
</div>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900">
        <div class="shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-lg bg-forest-100 dark:bg-forest-900/40 flex items-center justify-center text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300 text-sm font-bold">Zero</div>
        <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Zero Point</h3>
          <div class="text-yellow-500 text-xs mt-0.5">★★★★★</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-0.5">Pain management, overall relaxation</p>
        </div>
</div>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900">
        <div class="shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-lg bg-forest-100 dark:bg-forest-900/40 flex items-center justify-center text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300 text-sm font-bold">Eye</div>
        <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Eye Point</h3>
          <div class="text-yellow-500 text-xs mt-0.5">★★★★★</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-0.5">Eye strain, vision fatigue</p>
        </div>
</div>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900">
        <div class="shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-lg bg-forest-100 dark:bg-forest-900/40 flex items-center justify-center text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300 text-sm font-bold">Master</div>
        <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Master Cerebral</h3>
          <div class="text-yellow-500 text-xs mt-0.5">★★★☆</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-0.5">Headaches, concentration, memory</p>
        </div>
</div>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900">
        <div class="shrink-0 w-12 h-12 rounded-lg bg-forest-100 dark:bg-forest-900/40 flex items-center justify-center text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300 text-sm font-bold">Hunger</div>
        <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Hunger Point</h3>
          <div class="text-yellow-500 text-xs mt-0.5">★★★☆</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mt-0.5">Appetite control, cravings</p>
        </div>
</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Stimulate Ear Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The skin of the ear is thin, so use light fingertip pressure rather than squeezing or rubbing. Aim for roughly 90 degrees of pressure on each point and keep your neck and shoulders relaxed. Hold for 30 to 60 seconds for a quick session, or up to two minutes for a deeper feel, and work both ears so stimulation stays even. Breathe evenly and build pressure slowly over 10 to 15 seconds. Stop if the skin becomes sore, and never press on broken or infected skin.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do ear points work for the whole body?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">In the traditional model, the ear is a microsystem that mirrors the rest of the body, so each point is linked to a region or function elsewhere. The ear also receives branches of the vagus nerve and other cranial nerves, which may help explain why gentle pressure here can feel calming and grounding. No single mechanism is proven, and results vary from person to person, which is why ear acupressure is best treated as a supportive self-care practice.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I use ear seeds with acupressure?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Ear seeds are small balls, usually of vaccaria or magnetic material, held against the ear with tape. Many people combine them with hand pressure, pressing gently on the seed for 30 to 60 seconds several times a day. Keep the area clean and dry, remove the seed if it irritates the skin, and avoid placing seeds over any broken or infected areas. Consult a qualified practitioner if you are unsure how to place them.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For ear points, 30 to 60 seconds on each side is a reasonable starting session, and up to two minutes works well when you want a deeper, more settled feel. Press both ears so stimulation stays even. Work toward a comfortable deqi response—deep, dull, slightly aching or full, never sharp—and reduce pressure if it becomes uncomfortable. With very light pressure, a short session can be repeated a few times through the day.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 text-center">
    <a href="/atlas/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-forest-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-forest-300">Back to Body Atlas</a>
  </div>
</article>
</BaseLayout>